 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 515420,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "At a certain college, ID cards are issued to all students, faculty, and staff. These cards have unique ID codes for each person: a letter to indicate the person’s status (S for students, F for faculty, and E for staff), followed by 5 digits and finally 3 letters (these letters can be anything). How many different ID codes can be created using this scheme?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>The Multiplication Rule of Counting says that if there are <i>n</i> ways to accomplish one task and <i>m</i> ways to accomplish a second task, then there are <span data-math=\"n{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}m\"></span> ways to accomplish both tasks. You can tack on additional tasks by multiplying the number of ways to accomplish those tasks to your previous product.</p>\n<p>You have 3 choices for letters, 10 choices each time you choose a digit, and 26 choices for each time you choose a letter.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"3\\; \\times {\\text{ }}10{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}10{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}10{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}10{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}10{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}26{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}26{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}26{\\text{ }} = {\\text{ }}5,272,800,000\"></span></p>\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"3 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 26 \\times 26 \\times 26 = {\\text{5,272,800,000}}\">3 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 26 \\times 26 \\times 26 = 5,272,800,000</span>"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
